/* ============================================================================
   limit-of-rational-at-infinity.epher -- a ratio of quadratics tends to 3

   (3n^2 + 2n - 1)/(n^2 + 5) -> 3 as n -> infinity: dividing top and
   bottom by n^2 gives (3 + 2/n - 1/n^2)/(1 + 5/n^2), whose terms all
   die except the 3 (Stewart, "Calculus", 8th ed., sec. 2.6, limits of
   rational functions at infinity). The leading correction is 2/n, which
   is why n = 1e4 gives 3.0002 and n = 1e9 gives 3.000000002.

   At n = 10 and n = 100 the arithmetic is exact integer division, and
   epher shows the repeating decimals as fractions: 319/105 and 1313/435
   (3.038095... and 3.01999...). The display, not the math, differs.

   Accuracy: exact rational values at small n; values at large n round
   at about the 12th digit (2e-9 from the limit at n = 1e9).

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-rational-at-infinity.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\calculus\limits\limit-of-rational-at-infinity.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-rational-at-infinity.epher
   The knobs to edit are `ns` and `big` below.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs --------------------------------------------------------------
const ns = {10, 100, 10000, 1000000000}

// ---- the ratio at growing n, as a collected list ----------------------------
for n in ns do (3*n^2 + 2*n - 1)/(n^2 + 5)

// ---- the limit: the ratio of the leading coefficients, 3/1 ------------------
abs((3*ns[4]^2 + 2*ns[4] - 1)/(ns[4]^2 + 5) - 3) < 1e-6
3

/* ---- expected output ---------------------------------------------------------
= {10, 100, 10000, 1000000000}
= {319/105, 1313/435, 3.00019983999, 3.000000002}
= true
= 3
*/
